{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.SchenstedReverseRowIndex","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.SchenstedReverseRowIndex","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.SchenstedReverseRow"],"declarations":8,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.Schensted.rowUninsert_rightmost_witness_index","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.Schensted.rowUninsert_rightmost_witness_index","kind":"theorem","line":227,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SchenstedReverseRowIndex.lean#L227-L238","doc":"And the spec's index for that witness is `1`, not `0`: the row changed at index 1, the entry\nthere was `2`, and the one entry to its right is `≥ 3`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.Schensted.rowUninsert_rightmost_witness","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.Schensted.rowUninsert_rightmost_witness","kind":"theorem","line":225,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SchenstedReverseRowIndex.lean#L225-L225","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.Schensted.strictBelow_reverse_bump_step","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.Schensted.strictBelow_reverse_bump_step","kind":"theorem","line":171,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SchenstedReverseRowIndex.lean#L171-L217","doc":"**A reverse bump keeps the two rows column-strict.**\n\n`lower` gives up its entry `yLow` (writing `yBelow` in its place), `yLow` is reverse-inserted into\n`upper` (writing `yLow` in place of `yUp`), and the pair stays `StrictBelow`.\n\nFour column cases, and monotonicity kills the only bad one:\n\n* `j = kUp = kLow` — the upper row now reads `yLow`, the lower row now reads `yBelow`, and\n  `yLow < yBelow` because a reverse insertion always writes something strictly larger.\n* `j = kUp > kLow` — the lower row is unchanged at `j`, and `lt_of_index_lt` on the lower row's\n  maximality gives `yLow < lower[j]`.\n* `j = kLow < kUp` — the upper row is unchanged at `j`, and\n  `upper[kLow] < lower[kLow] = yLow < yBelow`.\n* neither — both rows unchanged at `j`.\n\n`j = kUp < kLow` is the case that would break it, and `reverse_bump_index_mono` excludes it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.Schensted.reverse_bump_index_mono","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.Schensted.reverse_bump_index_mono","kind":"theorem","line":149,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SchenstedReverseRowIndex.lean#L149-L164","doc":"**Reverse bump columns weakly increase going up** — the mirror of `bump_findIdx_mono`.\n\nIf the lower row gave up its entry at `kLow`, and the upper row sits strictly above it\n(`StrictBelow upper lower`), then reverse-inserting that entry into the upper row writes at an\nindex `kUp ≥ kLow`.\n\nThe proof is the whole reason column-strictness survives: `upper[kLow] < lower[kLow] = yLow`, so\n`kLow` is itself an eligible index in the upper row, and the write index dominates every eligible\nindex. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.Schensted.le_index_of_lt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.Schensted.le_index_of_lt","kind":"theorem","line":141,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SchenstedReverseRowIndex.lean#L141-L147","doc":"**The write index dominates every eligible index.** If any in-range entry of `r` is strictly\nbelow `y`, then `rowUninsert` writes at least that far right. Contrapositive of maximality. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.Schensted.lt_of_index_lt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.Schensted.lt_of_index_lt","kind":"theorem","line":120,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SchenstedReverseRowIndex.lean#L120-L129","doc":"**The displaced entry is strictly below everything to its right.**\n\nWorth noting how cheap this is: it does *not* need the row to be sorted. The maximality clause of\n`rowUninsert_spec` already says every entry right of `k` is `≥ y`, and the displaced `z` is `< y`,\nso the two chain directly. I expected to need weak sortedness plus a rightmost argument here; the\nspec hands it over. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.Schensted.rowUninsert_spec","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.Schensted.rowUninsert_spec","kind":"theorem","line":72,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SchenstedReverseRowIndex.lean#L72-L113","doc":"**What `rowUninsert` does, as an index.** See the module docstring: the fourth conjunct — that\nnothing right of `k` was eligible — is the maximality the column argument runs on. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.Schensted.getD_mem","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.Schensted.getD_mem","kind":"theorem","line":66,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SchenstedReverseRowIndex.lean#L66-L70","doc":"Reading an in-range position with `getD` lands in the list. `getD` is used throughout the\ncolumn machinery to dodge dependent-index proofs, so this is the bridge back to membership. ","signatureChunk":0}]}